Your password is stored in the format fml used before this version, and
should be changed.

That format keeps only the first eight characters of a password and
discards the rest.  However long the password you set was, eight of it
is in use, and those eight are all anyone has to find.

Changing it is the only way out of that.  fml will not do it for you:
the check that admitted you just now looked only at those same eight
characters, so re-storing what was typed would be re-storing eight
characters, and if they had been guessed rather than remembered it would
hand the account over.  The new password has to be one you supply in
full.

Once changed, it is stored hashed with PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 over a random
salt, and all of it is used.
